INT. TRAVELING CINEMATOGRAPH TENT - LATE AFTERNOON

1912. A canvas tent pitched in a wheat field. Bare benches, a sheet strung up for a screen, and sunlight leaking through the seams in thin gold blades. Smell of hot dust and machine oil.

MAGDALENA PRZYBYLSKA, forties, hands rough as bark, a leather apron over a plain gray dress, cranks the projector at the back of the tent. Slow. Even. A woman who has done this ten thousand times.

BARTHOLOMEW "BART" CROWLEY, fifties, thick-necked, in a checked waistcoat, paces the center aisle. He owns the tent. He owns the sheet. He thinks he owns Magdalena.

NIKODEM "NICK" LIPSKI, fifteen, thin, a cap too big for him, sits cross-legged on the front bench with a coil of film in his lap.

BART
(to the empty benches)
Doors in an hour. Not a seat taken yet.

MAGDALENA
It's harvest.

BART
Harvest is when they want a show.

MAGDALENA
Harvest is when they want sleep.

He doesn't answer. He stops beside the boy and flicks the brim of his cap.

BART
Nick. The reel.

NICK
Mending it.

BART
Mend faster.

Nick bends to his work. Magdalena's hand keeps turning the crank.

BART (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Three towns, no profit. Three towns.

MAGDALENA
Four, if you count the rain.

BART
I don't count the rain.

MAGDALENA
You should.

The sheet stirs. A breeze lifts it, and a bar of light slides across the benches and falls across Nick's face. He looks up, startled, then smiles at it.

Magdalena sees. She slows the crank a fraction.

MAGDALENA (CONT'D)
Nikodem. Come here.

He crosses the aisle. She lifts the lens cap, and the sheet fills with a dancing square of white.

MAGDALENA (CONT'D)
Hold your hand up.

He raises it. The light turns his palm to a pale map, the lines in it dark as rivers.

NICK
That's me.

MAGDALENA
That's you.

BART
What's this?

He has come up quietly behind them. Magdalena doesn't turn.

MAGDALENA
Teaching.

BART
I pay for projecting, not for teaching.

MAGDALENA
Then don't pay.

A long pause. Bart's ears go red.

BART
Nick. Back to your bench.

Nick looks at Magdalena. She nods once. He goes.

BART (CONT'D)
(low)
Don't spoil him.

MAGDALENA
Spoil him? He's the only one in this tent who looks at the picture.

Bart opens his mouth, closes it, and picks up the tin of film from the table. He tosses it from hand to hand, hard enough to ring.

BART
Doors in forty minutes.

MAGDALENA
We'll be ready.

He leaves. The flap swings shut behind him.

Magdalena exhales. She turns the crank again, slowly, and the white square on the sheet flickers into a picture: a train arriving at a station, small and silent and bright.

Nick comes back and sits in the front row. He doesn't speak.

NICK
(whispering)
Is it real?

MAGDALENA
It was. Once.

She keeps turning. The train keeps coming. In the field outside, a harvester sounds its horn, far away, and the light on the sheet pulses once in time with it.

NICK
Magdalena. Will you teach me the crank?

MAGDALENA
Not today.

NICK
When?

MAGDALENA
When he's gone.

He nods. Neither of them looks at the door. The train pulls in, and the little silent crowd on the sheet waves at nobody.

Magdalena lets the film run out. The sheet goes white, then gray, then plain canvas again.

She stands, rubs her shoulder, and for a moment, in the thin gold light, she looks fifteen years younger.

MAGDALENA (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Doors, then.
